#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134658} :activity/id #uuid "cf31c13c-070a-552d-91a4-1a67e47f24d1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048613 :topic/id #uuid "aeb25c88-ca51-51cd-bde9-b35abbd14a79" :topic/math-academy-id 2102} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135043 :tutorial/content "If a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to all the vectors in a set $W,$ we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is **orthogonal to $W,$** and we write $\\mathbf{u} \\perp W.$\n\nFor example, consider the set of vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ Can we find a *non-zero* vector $[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ that is orthogonal to both vectors in this set?\n\nWe know that if such a vector $\\mathbf u$ does exist, then the dot product of $\\mathbf u$ with each vector in the set will be zero:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo let's calculate these dot products:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the components of $\\mathbf{u},$ we just need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the system's matrix is already in echelon form and has $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we have $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_1=0.$ \n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, we have found a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\\mathbf{w}_2.$\n\n![](images/a6/a64d75a0f21a4238c4b297f890d4a3202917d831c9d80427cffcc75dcdf343a0.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "f7fff6c9-9086-5eef-b56a-a87ba95ad93b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3616 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dd572f85-b48d-5bec-90bb-6618a7fc0fc2" :step/math-academy-id 20219 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134659}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133587 :question/id #uuid "bbb751a9-ef0d-58cf-b788-18594dd67337" :question/math-academy-id "e-5560"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b03ea89-5152-5ff4-813a-ca985d60ed5b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049951}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348400}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonality"} :step/id #uuid "74cfe2c6-a806-59db-9604-de65c6177317" :step/math-academy-id 20220 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133588 :question/id #uuid "bbed6e9e-f63e-599f-ab8e-71cff46cfcfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5561"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9aa43961-5b6a-5c8b-adea-809d0a693cd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388709}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Components of a Vector Orthogonal to a Set"} :step/id #uuid "64ed6cee-8c85-5d4d-97ee-35ae15ad1e64" :step/math-academy-id 20221 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135044 :tutorial/content "Now, let's consider a *subspace* $H.$ The set containing all vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ that are orthogonal to $H$ is called the **orthogonal complement** of $H,$ and we denote it by $H^{\\perp}.$\n\nFor example, the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is the line that passes through the origin and is perpendicular to the plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb817f84f5a9f642b5721559f41c2c99a44f234cd22b8cf6e820b7f5d61e262.png)\n\nHow can we check whether a vector $\\mathbf v$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}?$ Sometimes, $H$ can contain many vectors (often infinitely many), so it may not be possible to check that $\\mathbf v$ is orthogonal to every individual vector in $H.$\n\nTo check whether a vector belongs to $H^{\\perp},$ we use the following theorem:\n\n> *A vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if it is orthogonal to a set of vectors that spans $H$. *\n\nIn other words:\n\n> *If $H=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\},$ then $\\mathbf{v} \\in H^{\\perp}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\}.$*\n\nFor example, if the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2,$ then the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of $H$ consists of all vectors that are perpendicular to both $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8cf96c9871966fb0a7a921b70229e4a17bb9f6dad1756986f0500c70be6ab95.png)\n\nSome other important things to notice about $H^{\\perp}$ are as follows:\n\n- $H^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\n- $(H^{\\perp})^{\\perp} = H$\n\n- $H \\cap H^{\\perp} = \\{\\mathbf 0\\}$" :tutorial/id #uuid "565f1aa6-6d92-5a0d-8035-62690c9f8e0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3617 :tutorial/title "Orthogonal Complements"} :step/id #uuid "261dd9cf-9401-5924-9e13-578c45a64439" :step/math-academy-id 20222 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134721}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133589 :question/id #uuid "ef531549-f5be-5002-b94f-1c7ee8682784" :question/math-academy-id "e-5562"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b2f7e06-54f8-5a9b-a926-362fb087b59c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395898}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Complement of a Span"} :step/id #uuid "c64ae3ad-aece-5b13-99de-d1ff9cb5fbfd" :step/math-academy-id 20223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133632 :question/id #uuid "b7cb0b20-e2a6-56d7-b568-ae08c259e581" :question/math-academy-id "e-5913"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb11d128-ee1b-5c35-aefb-1455570d20c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348416}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Which Vectors Belong to the Orthogonal Complement of a Set"} :step/id #uuid "cad6aad8-f1f1-51f6-82ae-549193d8d909" :step/math-academy-id 21872 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134662}}] :activity/title "Orthogonal Complements" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186133989}]}
